The system has three parts: frontend, backend, and cloud services.
Their responsibilities are as follows:

Frontend:
Display data including current conditions, AI summary, Forecast, AQI, Location (REQ-1.1, REQ-1.2, REQ-2.1, REQ-2.2, REQ-3.1, REQ-3.2, REQ-8.1).
Provide user interfaces for selecting a zip code, veiwing a weather map, and selecting units of measurement (REQ-4.1, REQ-5.1, REQ-6.1).
Store user preferences for units of measurement and saved locations, and a cache of the last weather data obtained (REQ-6.2, REQ-7.2, REQ-9.1).
Basic error handling including checking for an internet connection and validating that an entered zip code is five digits.

Backend:
Communicates between client device and cloud services
Store API keys
Handle more complex errors like request resend and timeout logic and errors thrown by cloud services such as invalid coordinates
Maintain a shared cache that can be used by many users to reduce API requests.

Cloud Services:
OpenWeatherAPI servers which provide weather data 
OpenAI API servers which provide LLM access

Each use case (UC) should be at least one class to decouple the different components.